# Response headers for STATIC ASSETS — build plan step 9.
#
# THIS FILE COVERS ONLY WHAT THE ASSETS BINDING SERVES, which is every route
# except `/`. The home page is the site's one non-prerendered route (step 7, so
# the strip can read KV), and a Worker-generated response never passes through
# this file — it is matched by src/middleware.ts instead. The two must be kept in
# step; there is no shared source, because one is text and the other TypeScript.
# `npm run build` will not catch a drift. The check that does is in ../README.md:
# curl `/` and `/about/` and compare.
#
# Astro middleware cannot do this job on its own: for a PRERENDERED route the
# middleware runs at BUILD time, so any header it sets is thrown away with the
# build — static files on disk carry no headers.
#
# NOT SET HERE, deliberately:
#
#   X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff — already served zone-wide by Cloudflare's
#   "No-Sniff Header" toggle, which lives INSIDE the HSTS panel (SSL/TLS → Edge
#   Certificates). Setting it here too would emit the header twice. Note the
#   coupling: turn HSTS off and nosniff goes with it, at which point it needs
#   adding to this file and to the middleware.
#
#   Content-Security-Policy — deferred past launch. The only scripts are the
#   JSON-LD data blocks and Cloudflare's edge-injected analytics beacon (which a
#   `script-src` would have to allowlist, or silently break), and the replay
#   figures and Astro's scoped styles would need a real audit before `style-src`
#   could be strict. A CSP that is 'unsafe-inline' everywhere buys close to
#   nothing.
#
#   Cache-Control on /_astro/* — the Cloudflare adapter INJECTS that rule into
#   this file during the build ("Injected immutable Cache-Control"). It skips the
#   injection if a matching rule already sets Cache-Control, so do not add one
#   here or the hashed assets lose their immutable year.

/*
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Cache for files served straight out of public/ — added 2026-09-05 after
# PageSpeed's "use efficient cache lifetimes" diagnostic, which was correct.
#
# Everything under /_astro/ is content-hashed by the build, so it gets a year and
# `immutable` (injected above). Files in public/ keep their LITERAL names, so a
# long cache is a promise you cannot take back: replace an image and returning
# visitors keep the old one until it expires, with no way to purge their browsers.
#
# Seven days is the deliberate middle. The post page alone carries 674 KB of
# these — eight times the whole home page — and before this they revalidated on
# EVERY visit, a network round trip per image on a phone. A week of caching takes
# that away while keeping the recovery window short, which matters here for one
# specific reason: the failure mode this site actually worries about is a
# redaction miss in a published screenshot, and that is precisely the case where
# "it will clear in a year" is the wrong answer.
#
# If an image must change sooner than its cache expires, RENAME IT. The URL is
# the cache key. Same discipline as the frozen post slugs, opposite direction.

/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
